WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:02.479
INTRO:
If you’ve ever watched a video about the

00:00:02.479 --> 00:00:08.320
stock market, you’ve likely heard the term
Nasdaq, but it’s often used in significantly

00:00:08.320 --> 00:00:09.820
different contexts.

00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:15.570
Sometimes, people are referring to an index
like the S&amp;P 500 or the Dow Jones.

00:00:15.570 --> 00:00:20.100
Othertimes, people are referring to a stock
exchange similar to the NYSE or the London

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:21.620
Stock Exchange.

00:00:21.620 --> 00:00:26.960
And in other cases, people are talking about
a publicly traded company called the Nasdaq.

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.230
Given all these different definitions, it’s
easy to write off the Nasdaq as just some

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:32.740
other gear in the financial markets.

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:37.800
But, the Nasdaq is actually the lifeblood
of the biggest tech companies in the world

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.100
including Apple, Microsoft, Amazon, Tesla,
Nvidia, and Google.

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:47.780
What’s even crazier is that the NASDAQ is
by no means a heritage player in the stock

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:48.780
market.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:52.540
In fact, the NASDAQ wasn’t even created
till 1971.

00:00:52.540 --> 00:00:57.450
For perspective, the NYSE was founded in 1792.

00:00:57.450 --> 00:01:02.219
Despite this short history, the NASDAQ has
become the king of the modern stock market.

00:01:02.219 --> 00:01:09.420
So here’s exactly what the NASDAQ is and
how it squashed legacy players to the top.

00:01:09.420 --> 00:01:13.980
COMPANY:
Starting with the origins of the NASDAQ, we

00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:16.330
have the company behind everything.

00:01:16.330 --> 00:01:21.430
The story of the company dates back to the
late 1960s when Wall Street was struggling

00:01:21.430 --> 00:01:23.510
to keep up with demand.

00:01:23.510 --> 00:01:28.360
This was because the 1960s was one of the
most prosperous times in the US.

00:01:28.360 --> 00:01:33.330
The economy was booming, wages were skyrocketing,
and GDP growth was through the roof.

00:01:33.330 --> 00:01:40.479
In fact, real GDP growth averaged 5% throughout
the 1960s and US payrolls increased by 32%.

00:01:40.479 --> 00:01:45.670
To top it all of, the US would reach the moon
near the end of the decade so the everyday

00:01:45.670 --> 00:01:50.540
American was feeling more confident than ever
before leading them to take a dabble with

00:01:50.540 --> 00:01:51.540
the stock market.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:52.540
At the time, the go to exchange to buy stocks
was the New York Stock Exchange or the NYSE.

00:01:52.540 --> 00:01:55.110
This drove unprecedented amounts of volume
into the NYSE.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:02:01.510
By the start of the 1970s, the average daily
trading volume was 12 million shares per day.

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:05.190
This might not sound like much compared to
the billions of shares that are traded on

00:02:05.190 --> 00:02:11.170
a daily basis today, but given that the NYSE
was only open for 5 hours per day in the early

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:16.420
1970s, we’re talking about executing 667
shares every single second.

00:02:16.420 --> 00:02:21.450
While this was great for business, the NYSE
had extremely difficulty handling all these

00:02:21.450 --> 00:02:22.450
trades.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:26.849
After all, they didn’t get any help from
computers, so they had to manually execute

00:02:26.849 --> 00:02:29.560
and keep track of every trade that took place.

00:02:29.560 --> 00:02:33.860
Seeing this rapidly compounding issue, the
National Association of Securities Dealers

00:02:33.860 --> 00:02:38.730
or the NASD decided to create an electronic
stock market called the NASDAQ or the National

00:02:38.730 --> 00:02:42.319
Association of Securities Dealers Automated
Quotations.

00:02:42.319 --> 00:02:46.250
And this gave rise to the world’s first
electronic stock market on February 8, 1971.

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:52.520
In the beginning, the NASDAQ wasn’t exactly
an electronic stock market per se given that

00:02:52.520 --> 00:02:55.450
they didn’t actually execute any trades
electronically.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:03:00.000
Rather, they were more of a quotation service
that allowed investors to electronically see

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.300
the live prices of the various stocks listed
on the platform.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:07.269
When it came to actually executing trades,
however, this was still done manually.

00:03:07.269 --> 00:03:12.720
But, it didn’t take long for the NASDAQ
to introduce electronic execution as well.

00:03:12.720 --> 00:03:18.190
STOCK EXCHANGE:
Over the next few decades, the NASDAQ introduced

00:03:18.190 --> 00:03:22.879
one feature after another from automatically
tracking daily volume to the electronic execution

00:03:22.879 --> 00:03:24.310
of trades.

00:03:24.310 --> 00:03:28.379
Given the relative ease of the NASDAQ stock
exchange, a lot of investors and especially

00:03:28.379 --> 00:03:30.269
traders flocked towards the exchange.

00:03:30.269 --> 00:03:36.519
By the early 1980s, the NASDAQ accounted for
37% of the entire US securities market and

00:03:36.519 --> 00:03:39.300
it wasn’t long before the crossed the 50%
mark.

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:44.290
But, despite the extreme popularity of the
NASDAQ, it was never that respected.

00:03:44.290 --> 00:03:49.720
In fact, the NASDAQ was often referred to
as an OTC market or an over-the-counter market.

00:03:49.720 --> 00:03:53.629
If you’re not familiar with this term, it
basically refers to the trading of stocks

00:03:53.629 --> 00:03:57.459
that aren’t listed on the major stock exchanges
such as the American Stock Exchange or the

00:03:57.459 --> 00:03:58.459
NYSE.

00:03:58.459 --> 00:04:03.500
The term OTC is generally used with a negative
connotation as it’s closely related to the

00:04:03.500 --> 00:04:05.900
trading of garbage penny stocks.

00:04:05.900 --> 00:04:09.849
Despite this negative association, some up
and coming tech companies like Microsoft and

00:04:09.849 --> 00:04:14.489
Apple who understood the power of electronic
trading started to embrace the NASDAQ.

00:04:14.489 --> 00:04:18.930
This didn’t change the perception of the
NASDAQ overnight, but it did cause investors

00:04:18.930 --> 00:04:21.409
to take the NASDAQ more seriously.

00:04:21.409 --> 00:04:25.229
After all, Microsoft and Apple were by no
means penny stocks.

00:04:25.229 --> 00:04:31.240
The Apple IPO, for example, minted 40 millionaires
and made Steve Jobs a centimillionaire.

00:04:31.240 --> 00:04:35.639
Throughout the rest of the century, the NASDAQ
dominated the tech IPO scene and they made

00:04:35.639 --> 00:04:37.909
ground breaking progress within the financial
markets.

00:04:37.909 --> 00:04:43.270
For example, in 1992, the NASDAQ partnered
with the London Stock Exchange which marked

00:04:43.270 --> 00:04:47.520
the first time that the capital markets of
different continents were linked together.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:51.680
But unfortunately, as the millennium came
to a close, so did the NASDAQ’s positive

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:53.949
reputation at least for the short term.

00:04:53.949 --> 00:04:59.349
You see, in the early 2000s, we saw one of
the most painful stock market crashes in history

00:04:59.349 --> 00:05:01.270
called the dotcom crash.

00:05:01.270 --> 00:05:05.729
Fortunately, everyday people weren’t really
affected by this crash and most of them don’t

00:05:05.729 --> 00:05:07.069
even know this took place.

00:05:07.069 --> 00:05:10.699
But, if you were invested in tech companies,
you got slaughtered.

00:05:10.699 --> 00:05:15.800
And given that basically every tech company
you can think of was listed on the NASDAQ,

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:19.919
the NASDAQ become associated with extreme
speculation and massive losses.

00:05:19.919 --> 00:05:24.069
While this wasn’t a great situation for
the NASDAQ, they continued to push forward

00:05:24.069 --> 00:05:26.819
the financial markets by leveraging the internet.

00:05:26.819 --> 00:05:31.440
In 1998, the NASDAQ became the first US exchange
to offer online trading.

00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.080
And the NASDAQ started using the slogan “the
stock market for the next hundred years.”

00:05:36.080 --> 00:05:40.370
The NASDAQ also started acquiring and partnering
with a bunch of foreign exchanges and they

00:05:40.370 --> 00:05:42.969
rebranded these as NASDAQ exchanges.

00:05:42.969 --> 00:05:47.530
This led to the rise of NASDAQ Copenhagen,
NASDAQ Stockholm, NASDAQ Helsinki, NASDAQ

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:49.619
Iceland, and so on and so forth.

00:05:49.619 --> 00:05:54.949
So, in the end, the company NASDAQ is simply
a conglomeration of stock exchanges from around

00:05:54.949 --> 00:05:55.949
the world.

00:05:55.949 --> 00:05:59.559
But, as we discussed in the beginning, this
is not the only thing that they’re known

00:05:59.559 --> 00:06:01.749
for.

00:06:01.749 --> 00:06:05.779
INDICES:
If people aren’t referring to the company

00:06:05.779 --> 00:06:10.520
or the exchange when the say NASDAQ, they’re
likely referring to the NASDAQ indices.

00:06:10.520 --> 00:06:14.969
Similar to the S&amp;P 500 and the Dow Jones,
the NASDAQ offers a few indices that have

00:06:14.969 --> 00:06:16.840
grown to be quite popular.

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:22.309
The two most popular NASDAQ indices are the
NASDAQ composite index and the NASDAQ 100.

00:06:22.309 --> 00:06:26.279
People often get confused between these two
and their nearly identical prices doesn’t

00:06:26.279 --> 00:06:30.399
exactly help with this situation, but there
is a major difference.

00:06:30.399 --> 00:06:35.439
The NASDAQ composite index was founded alongside
the company way back in 1971.

00:06:35.439 --> 00:06:39.909
And this index tracks all of the equity securities
that are listed on the NASDAQ stock market

00:06:39.909 --> 00:06:42.369
which includes over 2500 stocks.

00:06:42.369 --> 00:06:47.339
Initially, the index was launched with a price
of $100 and given that the NASDAQ composite

00:06:47.339 --> 00:06:53.360
currently stands at over $12,000, we’ve
seen a 120X return on this index since inception.

00:06:53.360 --> 00:06:58.069
For perspective, the S&amp;P 500 has grown 42X
during the same time period.

00:06:58.069 --> 00:07:02.979
Moving onto the NASDAQ 100, this is a conglomeration
of the 100 largest non-financial companies

00:07:02.979 --> 00:07:04.869
that are listed on the NASDAQ.

00:07:04.869 --> 00:07:09.809
The NASDAQ 100 wasn’t introduced till 1985,
but given that it’s not only filled with

00:07:09.809 --> 00:07:14.339
a bunch of tech stocks but also that it doesn’t
include any of the slowed moving financial

00:07:14.339 --> 00:07:18.779
stocks, I don’t think you’d be too surprised
to hear that the NASDAQ 100 has already produced

00:07:18.779 --> 00:07:20.159
a 100X return.

00:07:20.159 --> 00:07:24.969
But, it should also be noted that when we
experience a downturn, the NASDAQ 100 gets

00:07:24.969 --> 00:07:26.029
slaughtered.

00:07:26.029 --> 00:07:29.469
During the dotcom crash, for example, the
NASDAQ 100 sold of 83%.

00:07:29.469 --> 00:07:34.179
So, if you’re looking to invest into the
NASDAQ 100, you definitely need to be able

00:07:34.179 --> 00:07:35.770
to stomach some volatility.

00:07:35.770 --> 00:07:40.729
Anyway, the NASDAQ composite and the NASDAQ
100 aren’t the only two indices from the

00:07:40.729 --> 00:07:42.619
NASDAQ though they are the most popular.

00:07:42.619 --> 00:07:47.379
There’s also the NASDAQ Global Equity Index,
the NASDAQ Golden Dragon China Index, the

00:07:47.379 --> 00:07:52.119
PHLX semiconductor index, the OMX Stockholm
30 index and so on and so forth.

00:07:52.119 --> 00:07:56.580
Moving on from all these indices, the NASDAQ
also has their own stock.

00:07:56.580 --> 00:08:00.249
During the height of the dotcom bubble in
2000, the NASD decided to start selling their

00:08:00.249 --> 00:08:01.929
stake in NASDAQ.

00:08:01.929 --> 00:08:05.909
They started off with a private placement
offering, and shortly after, shares of the

00:08:05.909 --> 00:08:08.209
NASDAQ started trading on OTC markets.

00:08:08.209 --> 00:08:14.669
And eventually, on February 9, 2005, the company
NASDAQ was listed on the NASDAQ stock exchange.

00:08:14.669 --> 00:08:20.810
The ticker symbol for the company is NDAQ
and they currently sit at a $25 billion valuation.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:24.589
Clearly, embracing the digital world has played
out quite well for them.

00:08:24.589 --> 00:08:29.809
NASDAQ VS NYSE:
Alright so, the NASDAQ primarily rose to fame

00:08:29.809 --> 00:08:31.819
by embracing technology.

00:08:31.819 --> 00:08:35.529
First they embraced electronic trading and
then they embraced online trading.

00:08:35.529 --> 00:08:39.130
This attracted a bunch of tech firms to their
exchange and this is how they became super

00:08:39.130 --> 00:08:40.130
famous.

00:08:40.130 --> 00:08:45.500
But, nowadays, everyone has electronic and
online trading, so why do modern tech companies

00:08:45.500 --> 00:08:47.790
continue to choose the NASDAQ over the competition.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:52.290
Well, looking at it from a common sense perspective,
would you want to list your stock on an exchange

00:08:52.290 --> 00:08:55.899
that has lagged in innovation or would you
rather list it on an exchange that has lead

00:08:55.899 --> 00:08:56.930
the curve.

00:08:56.930 --> 00:09:01.819
The answer is obvious, but innovation is not
the only reason companies tend to go with

00:09:01.819 --> 00:09:03.100
the NASDAQ.

00:09:03.100 --> 00:09:05.170
Another major reason is cost.

00:09:05.170 --> 00:09:09.130
If you want to list 75 million shares on the
NYSE, you’re looking at an upfront $360,000

00:09:09.130 --> 00:09:11.730
plus an annual listing fee of $71,000.

00:09:11.730 --> 00:09:17.200
If you’re looking to list the same 75 million
shares on the NASDAQ capital market, however,

00:09:17.200 --> 00:09:22.310
you will be paying a slightly higher annual
fee of $79,000, but the upfront fee is significantly

00:09:22.310 --> 00:09:23.790
less at $75,000.

00:09:23.790 --> 00:09:28.670
At this rate, it would take 35 years for the
NYSE to become the cheaper option.

00:09:28.670 --> 00:09:32.970
Something else to consider is that the NYSE
also has more logistical requirements.

00:09:32.970 --> 00:09:37.350
For example, all companies listed on the NYSE
must have an independent compensation committee

00:09:37.350 --> 00:09:39.240
and an independent nominating committee.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:44.149
NYSE companies must also have an internal
audit function and corporate governance guidance.

00:09:44.149 --> 00:09:47.450
As you would guess, none of these factors
are requirements with the NASDAQ.

00:09:47.450 --> 00:09:52.330
So, if you’re simply looking at cost, logistics,
and innovation, NASDAQ is the easy choice

00:09:52.330 --> 00:09:53.330
to make.

00:09:53.330 --> 00:09:57.560
However, a select group of companies continue
to use the NYSE over the NASDAQ for a few

00:09:57.560 --> 00:09:59.959
reasons starting with prestige.

00:09:59.959 --> 00:10:05.430
As we discussed, the NASDAQ is often associated
with extreme speculation and insane volatility,

00:10:05.430 --> 00:10:08.100
and this association puts off a lot of investors.

00:10:08.100 --> 00:10:12.010
Also, it should be mentioned that the NYSE
goes out of their way to reduce volatility.

00:10:12.010 --> 00:10:17.980
According to the NYSE website, their exchange
is 51% less volatile at the open and 52% less

00:10:17.980 --> 00:10:19.660
volatile at the close.

00:10:19.660 --> 00:10:23.600
So, companies that are less growth oriented
and more stable like financial institutions

00:10:23.600 --> 00:10:27.980
and insurance companies often choose to list
on the NYSE over the NASDAQ.

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:32.600
This natural divide has made the NYSE the
go to place for value investors and the NASDAQ

00:10:32.600 --> 00:10:34.850
the go to place for growth investors.

00:10:34.850 --> 00:10:38.579
This doesn’t really matter for the end user
though given that both exchanges are offered

00:10:38.579 --> 00:10:41.240
at basically every brokerage you can think
of.

00:10:41.240 --> 00:10:46.680
NASDAQ EXPLAINED:
At the end of the day, none of the components

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:49.290
of the NASDAQ are particularly hard to understand.

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:53.589
The reason that it’s often confusing is
simply because the same name refers to so

00:10:53.589 --> 00:10:55.060
many different things.

00:10:55.060 --> 00:11:00.069
It would’ve been way better if each of them
had a different name, but unfortunately, this

00:11:00.069 --> 00:11:02.189
is the system we have.

00:11:02.189 --> 00:11:05.899
First there’s the NASDAQ company which is
charge of handling the NASDAQ exchange.

00:11:05.899 --> 00:11:10.230
Then, there’s the NASDAQ indices which primarily
include the NASDAQ composite index and the

00:11:10.230 --> 00:11:12.089
NASDAQ 100 index.

00:11:12.089 --> 00:11:15.829
And finally, there’s the stock of the NASDAQ
company itself.

00:11:15.829 --> 00:11:20.170
Over past few decades, the NASDAQ has proven
to be the more innovative exchange with much

00:11:20.170 --> 00:11:23.759
looser policies which has made them a favorite
amongst growth companies.

00:11:23.759 --> 00:11:29.000
But, a lot of companies in legacy industries
continue to prefer the NYSE and that’s the

00:11:29.000 --> 00:11:30.430
NASDAQ explained.

00:11:30.430 --> 00:11:35.199
If you were the owner of a multi-billion company,
would you list it on the NASDAQ or the NYSE?

00:11:35.199 --> 00:11:36.519
Comment that down below.

